WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.351 --> 00:00:04.600
BRIAN DORSEY: Hello, everyone.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.510
My name is Brian.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.250
I work on Google
Cloud, and we're

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:13.204
going to talk about where
should I run my code,

00:00:13.204 --> 00:00:14.620
deciding between
all these pieces.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:19.900
So I'm curious, how many
people here write code?

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:20.950
They OK.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:22.239
Lots of hands.

00:00:22.239 --> 00:00:22.780
This is what?

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.550
Maybe 80%?

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:29.180
How many people keep
systems running?

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:29.690
OK.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.570
Another maybe 30%, 40%.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:34.340
And how many people
tell other people

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:38.030
what to do, coordinate
work, that kind of thing?

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:38.530
OK.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.190
Another 20%, 30%.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:44.480
So lots of people here
do multiple things.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:45.510
Thank you for coming.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:49.070
And I think this will be
useful to all of those roles.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:53.750
So developer operations,
managers, altogether.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:58.470
So this question, where
should I run my code?

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:04.340
And the answer is, of
course, it depends.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.800
And so this talk will talk
about all the different ways

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:07.466
that it depends.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.646
At Google Cloud, we have
a lot of blue hexagons.

00:01:14.646 --> 00:01:16.910
You've seen a lot of
blue hexagons today.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:20.887
We're going to talk
mostly about these four,

00:01:20.887 --> 00:01:22.970
and I'm going to kind of
start from the bottom up.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:26.360
So Compute Engine are
our virtual machines.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.840
Container Engine is
hosted Kubernetes, so

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.270
orchestration for containers.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.210
App Engine is our
platform as a service.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.190
Give us code, we'll run it.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:40.230
And Cloud Functions are
kind of that internet glue.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.430
You just give a function
definition and it runs.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:48.019
So I'm going to make
a claim that you

00:01:48.019 --> 00:01:50.310
can use each of these things
for many different things.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.920
There's overlapping things.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.040
But there's a sweet spot,
kind of the best, happy

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.870
spot for each of these.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:02.960
And I claim that Compute
Engine is an excellent fit

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.130
for any existing system.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.400
So you've got a large
complicated system that

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.250
runs a lot of different pieces.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:14.660
It's probably running on virtual
machines or on hardware today,

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.330
and you can move that
over pretty much directly.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:21.290
If you have a system
that is containers first,

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.857
like you've got all Docker
images and your setup

00:02:23.857 --> 00:02:25.440
good to go and you
have a lot of those

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.590
you want to keep track of them,
Container Engine Kubernetes

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:31.140
is going to be a really
good fit for that.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:37.560
If your application is primarily
web, HTTP request response,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.720
App Engine is an excellent fit.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.010
And if you're trying to respond
to events, asynchronous events,

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.060
and you just don't want
to think about the rest,

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.810
Cloud Functions is awesome.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:50.160
So this is the claim
I'm going to make.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.260
And we're going to go,
like I said, bottom up.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:57.390
So VMs, containers,
paths, functions, and then

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.450
actually answer the
question, seriously,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.760
where should I run my code?

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.490
And then we're going to
have Elie from LumApps

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.530
come up and talk
about their decision

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:12.820
and how they decided between
these before they existed.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.590
So start off with
Compute Engine.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.310
And Compute Engine is
our virtual machines,

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.260
infrastructure as a service.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:28.150
It is compute, network, disk.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:29.910
We have all sorts of specifics.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:36.310
The machines can get
quite large, 96 GPUs--

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:42.310
sorry, 96 CPUs, eight GPUs,
lots of RAM, lots of disk.

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:47.350
And you can make a VM with each
of these almost independent.

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.020
So you can have a machine
with very large memory

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.750
and relatively small
CPU if that's what

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.150
you need for your application.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.910
They start very quickly,
about 30 seconds,

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:01.510
and we have lots of base
images already that you

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:05.170
can use to use the
operating system that you

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.950
are most familiar with, and then
you can make your own images

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:11.230
from those.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:15.890
I wanted to talk about
the disk a little bit.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:17.180
So these are virtual machines.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:19.060
We all know how
virtual machines work.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:22.240
Except, in the cloud, things
can work a little differently.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:29.810
So the disk, for example,
this, bigger disks

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:32.430
are faster in the sense
of more throughput.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:36.500
So if you're doing
any kind of testing--

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:38.420
like perf testing,
you want to see

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:41.240
what's going to be fast, is
this fast enough for you--

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:44.810
larger disks are actually
higher throughput

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:48.050
because you're
getting a slice of all

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.000
of these different disks
in the data center,

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.520
and a bigger disk gives
you a bigger slice.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.082
So you actually have more
physical disks working for you

00:04:56.082 --> 00:04:57.040
the larger the disk is.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.630
So that's maybe not obvious.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:05.570
And it's more than just VMs.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:10.040
So we have an optimized
OS that you can

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.180
use to just run one container.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.000
So if you want to
run a Docker image,

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.420
you can boot up a VM that's
set up for doing that.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.870
We have this system called
managed instance groups.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:22.640
So instead of thinking
about one computer,

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.060
you can think about a pool
of identical computers.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:29.000
And when you do
that, each of them

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.750
is created from
the same template,

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:33.470
and so you can make that
pool bigger or smaller.

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:35.560
And you just have
a dial for, I'd

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.900
like more computers doing
this processing or fewer.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.090
And that allows autoscaling.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:44.300
So you can hook that up
to traffic or CPQ metrics

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:49.550
and have it scale
the pool via those.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:53.640
Or, use that to spread
it across multiple zones.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.330
So if one zone goes
down, you still

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:59.480
have the same number of
computers running because it

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.570
will scale up in another zone.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:03.500
And you can use it
for rolling updates.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.480
If you have a new
version of the template,

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:09.290
you can actually drop one
machine off, add another one,

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.270
drop another one off,
add another new one,

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:15.260
and use that to kind of roll
to a new version of your base

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.300
image that you're using.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:19.070
And then, our load
balancer, when

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:22.040
you use Compute Engine or
Container Engine, App Engine

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:23.840
all the way up,
the load balancer

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.110
is actually configuring
Google's data centers.

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:29.060
So you're using the
same load balancer

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:33.450
we use for YouTube and
for Maps and the like.

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.840
So you get to
configure it and go.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.200
It's pretty fun.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.650
So when you're
using this, you are

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:42.650
thinking about your software.

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:44.510
You're thinking about
the operating system

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:49.160
you want to run, how much
memory, network, disk you need,

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:53.150
and the networking rules-- how
the pieces talk to each other.

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:55.070
All of that is things
you need to consider.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:07:00.110
And it's the things you're
used to thinking about in IT.

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:03.480
And there's APIs
for all of this.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.610
It is a particularly
good fit when

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:06.960
you have an existing system.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.209
You're going to kind of lift
something up and shift it

00:07:09.209 --> 00:07:11.640
to another place.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.590
This container-optimized
VM if you

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:18.180
want to just run one image, one
Docker image, it's very handy.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:21.780
If you need a very specific
kernel or very specific

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:25.860
operating system, you may
have license requirements

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.030
that push you--

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:30.240
like you're only
allowed to run, say,

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:35.240
four CPUs for the licensing
i the same machine.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.764
So you can use that.

00:07:37.764 --> 00:07:41.250
It's handy for running
databases and if you need very

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:42.630
specific network protocols.

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:48.910
We'll see some of the other
options only run HTTP, HTTPS.

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:52.000
Each of the VMs starts
in about 30 seconds,

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.730
so it sets your kind of scaling.

00:07:54.730 --> 00:08:00.400
You can run thousands of VMs
in like a minute to two minutes

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.840
time frame.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.060
And it's up to you to decide
how you update your software,

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:10.030
update your OS,
security patches.

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.570
All of that work is
still in your world.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:14.440
You have direct control
and responsibility

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:17.360
for managing that.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:18.010
So demo.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:19.040
Too much talking.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:20.730
Let's go and try something out.

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:26.320
So this-- yay, this is
Google Cloud Platform.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:31.694
Is relatively new?

00:08:31.694 --> 00:08:33.610
You haven't done too
much look at the website?

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:34.880
How many people?

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:35.380
OK.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:36.713
Maybe this won't be so exciting.

00:08:36.713 --> 00:08:41.220
But the secret to
Google Cloud Platform

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.169
is this hamburger button.

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:47.654
Everything is in the
hamburger button.

00:08:47.654 --> 00:08:49.820
That's where all of the
different features of Google

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:51.990
Cloud are.

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:56.030
And we will look at
the Compute Engine.

00:08:56.030 --> 00:09:00.360
When I actually have some
things set up for later demos

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:06.480
and we can create
a new instance,

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:12.870
I'm going to pick a nearby
European data center.

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:17.450
And we can, of course, choose
from some kind of setup,

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:21.790
preconfigured, sized machines.

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:25.890
Or-- sorry, wrong link--

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:29.360
click Customize,
and we get a slider

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:34.160
for how many CPU
cores do you want,

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:36.800
how much memory do you want.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:41.800
We can max them all
the way up, but I

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:43.820
have a specific size in mind.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:52.060
It's kind of what I think
of as maybe the answer.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.500
So that's the machine
we're going to build here.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:57.800
And also, this is
where you would

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.050
set if you wanted to
run GPUs and the like.

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:05.430
You can choose the
basic operating system.

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:06.980
I'm just going to
go ahead and use

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:09.997
one of the premade
Debian machines we have,

00:10:09.997 --> 00:10:12.080
but you can also kind of
pick from this whole list

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:17.090
of different images,
including Windows, SUSE,

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:20.610
Red Hat, and the like.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.800
So we'll hit Create.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.430
When I hit Create there, it's
actually made an HTTP request

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:31.560
off to the backend, and
it's allocating a new part

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.190
of a set of hardware somewhere.

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:36.450
It's connecting this disk
image to the machine,

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.010
booting the machine up.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.380
And then, if I had
set it up, it could

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:44.290
start to run my code
once it comes up.

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:45.720
Oh, hey, good.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:49.532
In demo fashion, it
finished quickly.

00:10:49.532 --> 00:10:51.490
And I just wanted to show
you this button here.

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:53.290
This is the SSH button.

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:57.660
And what this does is, since I'm
logged in to the web interface,

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:01.020
I have already done an
OAuth authentication.

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:02.760
Google knows who
I am, and it will

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:07.920
push SSH keys to the instance
so that we can log in over SSH.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.560
And you can do this from the
command line or from the web.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:15.430
And it's a real computer.

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:20.530
We can prove that
it's a real computer

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:23.930
because we have scrolling text.

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:26.540
Thank you for laughing at that.

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:29.660
But basically, you can
SSH right from the web.

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:35.970
So that's like a really
quick tour of Compute Engine.

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:38.760
So why might you
choose Compute Engine?

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:40.200
Consistency.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.930
If you do a test with
10 VMs, next week

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:45.500
you should see the same
behavior, the same performance

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:50.370
with 10 VMs, or in another
region the same behavior.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:52.200
You can choose exactly
the shape of VM,

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.810
and this is really handy
for existing systems.

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:56.370
Instead of trying
to optimize or get

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:57.995
a bunch of different
pieces of software

00:11:57.995 --> 00:12:00.870
to work on the same
machine because it happens

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:04.170
to have 128 gigs of RAM,
you can give one machine

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:07.200
32 and the other
96 and just keep

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.840
them separate with exactly how
much they need to be happy.

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:15.800
I will guess that
everyone in here

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.020
has had a computer
run out of disk

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:22.370
and fail at some
point in their life.

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:25.970
You can actually resize disks
that are attached to VMs

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:29.360
and make them larger and
then update the partition.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:32.300
In most OSs, update all
the way through the OS

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:35.210
so it sees the additional
space and actually give it

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:37.730
space while it's
alive, which is fun.

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:41.900
And then our billing is set
up to be as easy as possible

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:45.830
to reason about, to understand,
automatically give you

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:50.210
discounts when you use it
over time, that sort of thing.

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:52.250
We also move these VMs.

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:57.620
If we have planned updates
to the hardware or the host

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:00.140
operating systems, we'll
actually move the VMs

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:02.000
to another machine.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:04.290
And it's almost
unnoticeable, usually

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:07.637
in the single-digit
millisecond pause.

00:13:07.637 --> 00:13:09.970
All the network traffic makes
it through, comes back up,

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:13.970
it's actually on new
hardware, and we continue on.

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:17.650
And it's a really natural fit
for many existing systems.

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:20.510
So Compute Engine, it's awesome.

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:24.320
And if you're using Container
Engine or App Engine Flex,

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:26.060
all of those are
actually running--

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:29.220
we use Compute Engine as the
base for running these as well.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:31.380
So all of the benefits
I talked about so far,

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:34.110
you get in these
other tools as well.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:38.320
So next, kind of we're
going to keep going.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:40.940
Container Engine.

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:42.380
More questions.

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:45.710
How many people are using
containers, Docker, something

00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:47.050
like that already?

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:47.550
OK.

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:50.310
That's maybe 40%,
50% of the room.

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:52.410
That's awesome.

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:55.620
And I'm going to just assume
everybody else is interested,

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:58.170
and so we're all in.

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:00.570
So the short version
of containers, why?

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:03.090
It solves the, well, it
worked on my machine,

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:08.250
why doesn't it work in
production problem very nicely.

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:10.050
It also gives you
a nice isolation

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:13.300
between different processes
so they can't hurt each other.

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:14.910
So they have all
their dependencies,

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:17.590
and they're kind of
insulated from each other.

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:20.850
And this enables all
sorts of good things

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:22.620
that you're probably aware of.

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:25.561
Once you decide you're
all in on containers,

00:14:25.561 --> 00:14:27.560
then you could end up
with hundreds or thousands

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:30.380
of containers, and that's
where Kubernetes comes in.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:34.340
And it is an open-source
orchestration system

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:37.670
for managing lots of containers.

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:44.030
It lets you treat a cluster
of machines as one thing.

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:46.400
And you say, run this
for me, and the cluster

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:48.080
figures out where to put it.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:51.530
And if one of them goes
down, it figures out

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:52.610
where to start a new one.

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:55.830
So it keeps things set up
the way you want it to be.

00:14:55.830 --> 00:15:02.300
It's open source,
owned by foundation.

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:05.480
Just a few days ago,
Docker announced

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:07.130
that they are
including Kubernetes

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:09.640
in their distribution
in the future.

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:13.130
It is really worth looking at.

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:16.060
So when you're using Kubernetes,
what do you think about?

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:18.890
Your thinking about
your application.

00:15:18.890 --> 00:15:22.310
When you get into a meeting
room with your colleagues,

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:23.900
you've got a white
board, and you're

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:26.030
drawing lines and boxes
between the different parts

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:27.290
of the system.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:31.100
And Kubernetes actually
lets you reason

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:35.450
about your system in a very
similar level of abstraction.

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:37.070
When you're making
a config file,

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:41.000
you say, this component, I
want five of them running.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:44.120
And it uses this
other piece by name,

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:46.870
and Kubernetes figures out how
to connect them to each other,

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:48.170
how to do the networking.

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:53.210
So you get to live at this kind
of white-board level thinking

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:56.150
and push the configuration
down to the computers

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:58.320
at that level of abstraction.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:00.830
So that's really powerful.

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:04.700
Container Engine is Google
Cloud's hosted version

00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:05.540
of Kubernetes.

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:07.540
So we run the cluster for you.

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:11.900
We upgrade, keep the
nodes up to date,

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:14.180
update to new
versions of the host,

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:18.480
and scale the size of the
cluster for you up and down.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:21.840
It's a very good fit
for any situation

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:24.330
where you need to insulate
yourself from the environment.

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:27.990
So you want to run the
same application, say,

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:30.570
on your own machines
or in a cloud,

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:33.630
in another cloud
in China and you

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:36.270
need it to work the same way.

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:39.170
It's also very useful to
have consistent behavior

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:44.600
through dev, test,
production, and basically

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:46.310
get high utilization
out of your systems

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:50.120
because you can have more
code running on them.

00:16:50.120 --> 00:16:53.106
It's a really good
fit when you know

00:16:53.106 --> 00:16:54.730
you're going to use
lots of containers,

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:57.370
so you can take full
advantage of them

00:16:57.370 --> 00:17:00.299
and the ephemeral nature.

00:17:00.299 --> 00:17:01.840
I'm going to say
it's a good fit for,

00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:04.630
but this is also kind
of a requirement,

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:07.780
of needing good
team communication.

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:11.920
So between your dev, your
ops, and your security teams,

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:15.640
because the code is
deployed in these images,

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:18.280
if you want to make a
security fix to that code,

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:20.500
you need to be in
the same pipeline

00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:21.800
that the developers are using.

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:25.180
Make the changes in
a compatible way.

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:28.540
So probably, you want a
continuous integration system

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:30.520
of some kind, a
build system, and you

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:33.340
want good communication
between the different parts

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:36.150
of your organization.

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:38.380
It requires you
to use containers.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:42.030
So it is only for
containerized software.

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:44.520
And if you need things
like per machine licensing,

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:47.950
it can get a little
challenging and the like.

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:50.820
So that's where.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:53.410
The quickie version--

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:58.260
I'm going to skip,
no, we'll do it--

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:00.910
of this.

00:18:00.910 --> 00:18:04.530
So this is the overall
UI for Container Engine.

00:18:04.530 --> 00:18:08.970
Because this is a talk, this
is kind of like a cooking show.

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:11.010
I've prepared the
cluster already.

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:12.690
It's already running.

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:15.910
And we click on this.

00:18:15.910 --> 00:18:17.910
There's some information
about the cluster here,

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:19.534
but the main thing
I wanted to show you

00:18:19.534 --> 00:18:21.650
is down here, instance groups.

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:24.630
It's actually using
the managed instance

00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:28.020
groups of Compute Engine to
set the size of the pool.

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:30.570
So we're just using regular
Compute Engine features

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:32.390
to do that.

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:35.150
There is a dashboard built
into the open-source Kubernetes

00:18:35.150 --> 00:18:36.680
tool.

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:38.480
It looks like this.

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:41.030
And I currently have
nothing deployed

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:43.610
behind this load balancer.

00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:48.360
So there's a public
IP address here,

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:51.220
which I guess if it rewrites
quickly you could look at.

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:53.960
And there's nothing
running there right now.

00:18:53.960 --> 00:19:00.010
So we can quickly say queue CTL
run, something named Engine X,

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:03.700
based off an image, a
Docker Image, Engine X.

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:08.624
And this is a web server
that's very commonly used.

00:19:08.624 --> 00:19:09.790
And that's already deployed.

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:14.420
So if we take a look over
here, we actually already

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:16.654
have this thing called
a deployment, a pod.

00:19:16.654 --> 00:19:18.320
I'm not going to go
into names too much,

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:23.780
but this pod is one
process, running Engine X.

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:25.700
And in fact, over
here in this window,

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:28.640
we're already seeing that
network connection actually

00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:33.800
finished and we got the
default web serving page up.

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:43.560
We can also scale
and then say we

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:49.170
want to actually have,
say, 12 of these.

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:54.410
And by the time I get
back over here to refresh,

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:57.020
I didn't even catch it
with some of them starting.

00:19:57.020 --> 00:19:59.500
They're actually all
running, up and going.

00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:02.603
So that's a really quick
feel for Kubernetes.

00:20:06.680 --> 00:20:08.090
So we talked about these things.

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:13.300
Basically, it's very
useful to insulate you

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:15.780
from your other infrastructure.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:20.950
It enables higher utilization
so that the more processes you

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:23.500
have running, the higher
percentage of the CPU

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:27.040
you are using, the less money
you are spending overall.

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.950
And it is a really
nice abstraction spot

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:31.930
for many folks.

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:32.930
OK.

00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:35.600
Next up is App Engine.

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:39.050
So App Engine is our
platform as a service.

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:43.670
It lets developers focus on
the code they're writing,

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:46.550
and then we handle the rest.

00:20:46.550 --> 00:20:49.880
I think we could say it
was serverless early on.

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:55.190
So App Engine has been around
for six, seven years now--

00:20:55.190 --> 00:20:58.640
actually, started
in 2008, so more.

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:01.040
And the trade-off App
Engine has always been,

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:04.230
if you write your app
in this particular way,

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:06.860
then give us the code and
we'll scale it up and down

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:10.400
from zero traffic to millions
of requests per second.

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:13.820
And we can make that happen.

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:17.330
Just recently, we've
announced and released

00:21:17.330 --> 00:21:19.280
the App Engine
flexible environment,

00:21:19.280 --> 00:21:21.170
which takes the good
stuff from App Engine

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:24.410
and lets you run any
Docker image and have

00:21:24.410 --> 00:21:26.690
that managed by App Engine.

00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:31.520
So you can take and run whatever
language you want to use,

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:34.370
any specific C
libraries you need--

00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:36.530
these things that were
kind of limitations

00:21:36.530 --> 00:21:38.220
in the previous
version of App Engine--

00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:39.200
and you can run them.

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:41.060
And it also means
you can kind of

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:43.370
take advantage of the
containerization you've

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:45.800
been doing in other places.

00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:49.770
So pretty much, if it runs
in a Docker container,

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:52.630
you can run it on
App Engine Flex.

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:56.550
When you're using App Engine,
you're thinking about the code

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:59.290
that you've written
in Python or Go--

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:01.120
whatever language you're using--

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:06.020
the HTTP requests and responses,
and the version of your code.

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:07.070
And that's about it.

00:22:07.070 --> 00:22:11.470
There's no servers that
you need to worry about.

00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:16.120
You can focus directly
on your business needs.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:18.070
As I said before, it's
a really good fit.

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:20.970
It's meant for
serving websites--

00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:23.950
websites, or HTTP APIs--

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:26.680
you know, anything
that's HTTP based.

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:28.750
Scales very well
to high traffic.

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:32.620
The standard version, we
have run times for several

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:37.030
languages, but you cannot add
your own binary extensions

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:37.900
to it.

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:40.840
And then the Flex version,
basically anything

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:43.990
you can do in Docker
you can do there.

00:22:43.990 --> 00:22:47.440
Today, it's not the
best fit for sites

00:22:47.440 --> 00:22:49.570
that go down to
almost no traffic

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:52.210
because Flex has a
minimum of two VMs.

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:55.150
So you end up paying for
two VMs under the covers

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:59.390
all the time, whereas standard
will scale down to zero and pay

00:22:59.390 --> 00:23:00.640
nothing if there's no traffic.

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:08.060
So a quickie demo of App Engine.

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:10.580
So this is the App Engine
dashboard for a site

00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:14.730
that I have that was made by
a colleague, Justin Beckwith,

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:18.200
and it serves up
an image of fruit.

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:20.890
It's the whole website.

00:23:20.890 --> 00:23:23.982
But there are multiple
versions of this website

00:23:23.982 --> 00:23:25.690
because some people
like different fruit.

00:23:25.690 --> 00:23:29.150
And we've argued about
this a little bit.

00:23:29.150 --> 00:23:30.790
We did some user
research to figure out

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:32.831
what the most popular fruit is.

00:23:32.831 --> 00:23:33.830
Let's do some right now.

00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:37.300
How many people
here like durian?

00:23:37.300 --> 00:23:39.490
Actually, I'm not
even sure that's

00:23:39.490 --> 00:23:44.150
an English word much
less translates over.

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:46.040
Let's go ahead and
switch the site

00:23:46.040 --> 00:23:49.130
because we know durian
is a very popular fruit.

00:23:49.130 --> 00:23:53.570
We're going to actually migrate
all the traffic from this site

00:23:53.570 --> 00:23:55.310
to a new version
of the website that

00:23:55.310 --> 00:23:57.790
serves up a different fruit.

00:23:57.790 --> 00:24:03.702
And if I refresh the page,
does that look familiar?

00:24:03.702 --> 00:24:04.660
What is that in French?

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:12.640
I didn't quite hear that,
but it's awesome maybe.

00:24:12.640 --> 00:24:15.950
I think people love
or hate durian.

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:18.230
But we can switch the
version that quickly,

00:24:18.230 --> 00:24:20.450
and you can roll back
to a previous version.

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:22.460
If we want to go back
to cherry because I

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:25.070
was wrong about
how popular it is,

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:28.890
we can revert back to a
previous version very easily.

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:31.670
And we can also kind
of split the traffic,

00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:40.680
where we say we're going to
do 80% apple and 22% durian,

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:43.270
or whatever we'd like to do.

00:24:43.270 --> 00:24:46.060
So that's a fun thing
you can do because you've

00:24:46.060 --> 00:24:47.930
got the hosted environment.

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:48.430
OK.

00:24:48.430 --> 00:24:52.840
So App Engine lets your
developers focus on code,

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:55.210
and we handle all the
rest that you need to do.

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:57.700
So that's awesome.

00:24:57.700 --> 00:25:00.220
And if you add in the
flexible runtimes,

00:25:00.220 --> 00:25:04.000
that means you can
run any Docker image

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:07.760
and you can also take that
image and run it other places.

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:11.230
So this same code that you've
made to run the App Engine,

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:14.860
you can run in Container
Engine or on virtual machines

00:25:14.860 --> 00:25:21.250
or on your own
machines actually.

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:23.860
And even though this
is fully managed,

00:25:23.860 --> 00:25:27.480
you can actually still SSH in
and do debugging and things

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:30.480
if you need to via Flex.

00:25:30.480 --> 00:25:33.790
And just for fun, App
Engine is used a lot.

00:25:33.790 --> 00:25:37.990
100 billion requests per
day on average last time we

00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:39.220
updated the numbers.

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:42.700
So next up-- we're just
going to keep going--

00:25:42.700 --> 00:25:44.380
Cloud Functions.

00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:49.870
So this is internet
glue, I'm going to say.

00:25:49.870 --> 00:25:55.392
And I think you can use it
in lots of different ways,

00:25:55.392 --> 00:25:57.600
but I think one of the most
powerful, kind of easiest

00:25:57.600 --> 00:25:59.880
places to start is for
things that are event driven.

00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:01.170
Something happens.

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:04.450
You want to do some
processing, do something else.

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:06.930
So event, process.

00:26:06.930 --> 00:26:11.020
Think of it as it just
starts a function call. .

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:14.010
So the events we
can trigger off of

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:19.770
are Pub/Sub, Cloud Storage,
and a straight HTTP request.

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:23.440
And this isn't actually
the topic of the talk,

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:26.940
but if you're using
Google Cloud I highly

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:30.100
recommend looking into
Pub/Sub and Cloud Storage.

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:36.990
They're kind of like the glue
between the blue hexagons.

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:39.810
Almost every application uses
Cloud Storage and Pub/Sub

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:40.965
in some fashion.

00:26:40.965 --> 00:26:42.610
It's very, very useful.

00:26:42.610 --> 00:26:45.540
So please check them out.

00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:47.970
Cloud Functions
is fully managed.

00:26:47.970 --> 00:26:49.620
You have the
function definition,

00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:53.190
and you just push that
and we run it for you.

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:57.490
And you only pay per execution.

00:26:57.490 --> 00:26:59.910
It is an open kind of format.

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:01.860
We have a Node.js
JS runtime that you

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:04.080
can run on your own
machines or you can

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.120
have hosted in Cloud Functions.

00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:11.204
So you can do local testing
and run it in other places.

00:27:11.204 --> 00:27:12.870
So when you're using
it, you're thinking

00:27:12.870 --> 00:27:15.660
about events and the
function definition,

00:27:15.660 --> 00:27:18.290
and that's kind of it.

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:21.440
It's a good fit when you want
to be serverless upfront.

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:24.362
If you're already using
Pub/Sub or Storage,

00:27:24.362 --> 00:27:26.570
you don't want to think
about the runtime environment

00:27:26.570 --> 00:27:27.830
very much.

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:31.050
And it's basically this glue.

00:27:31.050 --> 00:27:33.560
Constraints wise,
I'll very carefully

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:40.530
say there is one runtime
today, and that's Node.js, so

00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:44.300
JavaScript on Node.js.

00:27:44.300 --> 00:27:46.510
Looking at other languages.

00:27:46.510 --> 00:27:50.170
And it forces you to
think per function.

00:27:50.170 --> 00:27:52.330
And sometimes, that's
a real advantage.

00:27:52.330 --> 00:27:54.340
And sometimes, that
can get complicated

00:27:54.340 --> 00:27:56.080
if you have lots of functions.

00:27:56.080 --> 00:27:59.090
So kind of be careful there.

00:27:59.090 --> 00:28:01.870
And demo is less of
a demo, more kind

00:28:01.870 --> 00:28:05.240
of just what this looks like.

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:09.970
This is the Cloud
Functions overview here.

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:10.970
Let me actually go back.

00:28:10.970 --> 00:28:14.640
So this is a list of different
functions I have deployed.

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:18.610
I've got a hello world function
that, as you might expect,

00:28:18.610 --> 00:28:19.750
doesn't do very much.

00:28:22.790 --> 00:28:27.480
It has an HTTP URL that
we can use to trigger it.

00:28:27.480 --> 00:28:31.980
So if I click on that,
it will run it and do

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:33.420
whatever the function does.

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:35.190
We gave it no parameters.

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:37.530
And this function, if you
give it no parameters,

00:28:37.530 --> 00:28:40.810
just says no message, I don't
know what to do with that.

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:43.470
So what does this
function look like?

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:46.500
We can come and look
at the source here.

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:50.370
And we'll find out that,
if there's no body,

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:53.220
it just returns this
message we just saw.

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:55.890
And if there is something,
it will actually

00:28:55.890 --> 00:28:58.140
log that and return.

00:28:58.140 --> 00:29:00.752
So it's kind of like
an echo hello world.

00:29:00.752 --> 00:29:01.930
app.

00:29:01.930 --> 00:29:07.980
So if I grab this, we can
actually come over here.

00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:11.540
So you can do testing via
HTTP any way you like,

00:29:11.540 --> 00:29:13.980
but there's also
kind of a built-in,

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:16.550
Let's just try this out tab.

00:29:16.550 --> 00:29:20.690
So we can say, hello Paris.

00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:21.960
Click Test the Function.

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:23.690
It will make that call.

00:29:23.690 --> 00:29:26.820
We actually get a success back,
and that's what it looks like.

00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:30.320
So there isn't that much
to see in Functions.

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:32.480
But basically, you
take some input.

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:35.510
You can do some work,
have a side effect or not,

00:29:35.510 --> 00:29:39.780
and then return some
information back at the end.

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:42.290
So this is just a function
that lives in the cloud,

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:45.860
and you can use it to
connect different pieces.

00:29:45.860 --> 00:29:48.587
It's integrated with our logging
system, centralized logging.

00:29:48.587 --> 00:29:50.420
So you can look at it
here, but you can also

00:29:50.420 --> 00:29:52.580
look at it in
Stackdriver Logging,

00:29:52.580 --> 00:29:56.518
see what's happening with
them, that sort of thing.

00:29:56.518 --> 00:29:57.940
OK.

00:29:57.940 --> 00:30:00.677
So you think about Functions.

00:30:00.677 --> 00:30:02.260
You don't have to
think about servers.

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:05.740
You just pay for what you use,
simple developer experience.

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:06.280
OK.

00:30:06.280 --> 00:30:10.780
Seriously, where
should I run my code?

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:12.160
Did you find a good fit already?

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:13.810
So as I was talking
through things,

00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:16.480
did Compute Engine or
Container Engine jump out?

00:30:16.480 --> 00:30:20.680
If so, go take a look at that,
research that, play with that,

00:30:20.680 --> 00:30:23.180
see what it looks like.

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:26.490
Probably, though, it
depends a little bit.

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:30.140
So here's a few more things that
you might consider in addition

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:34.280
to just what these tools are.

00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:38.150
The level of abstraction
that it gives you,

00:30:38.150 --> 00:30:41.690
the technical requirements
of your application,

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:43.400
and the team that you have--

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:45.566
your company, the
people you work with,

00:30:45.566 --> 00:30:46.440
what those look like.

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:52.960
So for example, for
abstraction, what happens is,

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:55.660
what level do you want
to be thinking at?

00:30:55.660 --> 00:31:01.410
So if you have people that are
more developer kind of mindset,

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:03.930
you might want to spend more
time thinking about code.

00:31:03.930 --> 00:31:07.720
If you have a really
established operations group,

00:31:07.720 --> 00:31:12.600
you may be already wanting to
think about containers or down

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:14.796
into Compute Engine.

00:31:14.796 --> 00:31:17.030
So abstraction level.

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:18.750
Technical requirements.

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:21.950
So if you have specific
needs, if you need a custom

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:24.950
kernel to make your
application run,

00:31:24.950 --> 00:31:26.600
you're probably
going to be looking

00:31:26.600 --> 00:31:30.190
at VMs down at Compute Engine.

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:33.650
And so technical
constraints may kind of

00:31:33.650 --> 00:31:37.850
push you down the
abstraction stack,

00:31:37.850 --> 00:31:39.460
something that happens there.

00:31:39.460 --> 00:31:41.060
And then your team.

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:43.820
How do they think
about software?

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:47.690
How comfortable are they
with containers, for example?

00:31:47.690 --> 00:31:50.420
How much existing code
do you have that you

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:51.630
need to make sure it works?

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:53.690
And do you have
processes that need

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:56.910
to work a very specific
way in your company,

00:31:56.910 --> 00:32:00.030
and you may need more
flexibility to implement those?

00:32:00.030 --> 00:32:03.400
That may kind of push you
down into the details a bit

00:32:03.400 --> 00:32:05.220
sometimes.

00:32:05.220 --> 00:32:05.814
OK.

00:32:05.814 --> 00:32:07.230
So all these
different things, how

00:32:07.230 --> 00:32:09.570
do you make the right decision?

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:12.470
I'm going to claim, no worries.

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:17.900
It's actually very easy to
switch between these levels

00:32:17.900 --> 00:32:20.150
if you're in containers.

00:32:20.150 --> 00:32:23.360
So that's the kind of if there.

00:32:23.360 --> 00:32:28.650
So if your application can
run in a Docker container,

00:32:28.650 --> 00:32:29.570
then awesome.

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:31.522
And if you start at
the App Engine level--

00:32:31.522 --> 00:32:33.980
so if you're starting a new
project and you start at a very

00:32:33.980 --> 00:32:36.060
high level of abstraction--

00:32:36.060 --> 00:32:39.350
if you want to move down,
it's a change one config line

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:42.440
and you go from App
Engine Standard to Flex.

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:45.560
Once you're in Flex, it
creates a Docker image

00:32:45.560 --> 00:32:47.090
every time you deploy.

00:32:47.090 --> 00:32:48.680
So you can take that
same Docker image

00:32:48.680 --> 00:32:50.427
and you can run that
in Container Engine

00:32:50.427 --> 00:32:52.010
or you can run that
in Compute Engine.

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:53.690
So you can take
the exact same code

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:56.390
and run it in all of these
environments already.

00:32:56.390 --> 00:33:00.110
If you're starting at Compute
Engine and need to go up,

00:33:00.110 --> 00:33:03.950
there's some work involved in
getting it into containers.

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:05.660
And then, you can
do the same thing.

00:33:05.660 --> 00:33:07.250
You can kind of
move up the stack

00:33:07.250 --> 00:33:10.370
and decide where
you want to run it.

00:33:10.370 --> 00:33:14.150
Also, most systems
that are mature, they

00:33:14.150 --> 00:33:15.800
end up using multiple
parts of things.

00:33:15.800 --> 00:33:19.580
You might have an App Engine
frontend for your web API

00:33:19.580 --> 00:33:21.830
and some long-running
processes--

00:33:21.830 --> 00:33:23.840
maybe you do transcoding,
video transcoding,

00:33:23.840 --> 00:33:25.610
or some other work--

00:33:25.610 --> 00:33:26.756
in VMs.

00:33:26.756 --> 00:33:28.880
So it's very common to have
a system that's made up

00:33:28.880 --> 00:33:32.720
of using these together.

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:33.400
OK.

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:37.820
So back to the very
beginning, if you've

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:41.920
had an event-driven problem,
start at Cloud Functions.

00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:43.870
If you are starting
a new thing, you

00:33:43.870 --> 00:33:47.290
know you're going to be on the
web, start with App Engine.

00:33:47.290 --> 00:33:52.130
If you're all in on containers,
Kubernetes, Container Engine.

00:33:52.130 --> 00:33:54.530
And if you have an
existing system,

00:33:54.530 --> 00:33:58.370
you should probably start
looking at Compute Engine.

00:33:58.370 --> 00:34:00.860
And that is the core
of that, and I would

00:34:00.860 --> 00:34:03.740
like to hand over to Elie.

00:34:03.740 --> 00:34:06.430
And this clicker for you.

