00:36:09.770 --> 00:36:14.780
So just to give you a bit more
information about our story,

00:36:14.780 --> 00:36:19.510
so we created the
company in 2012.

00:36:19.510 --> 00:36:22.490
So at the beginning,
it was just three guys.

00:36:22.490 --> 00:36:25.160
We were not in a garage
because in France people

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:27.980
work in an office.

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:31.910
And we started at a time
where the Google Cloud

00:36:31.910 --> 00:36:35.130
Platform did not even exist.

00:36:35.130 --> 00:36:37.700
So at that time, it
was called App Engine,

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:40.680
and it was a very,
very new product.

00:36:40.680 --> 00:36:43.140
But when we started,
we didn't have

00:36:43.140 --> 00:36:46.050
the necessary
financial resources

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:51.030
nor the time nor the people to
rely on our own infrastructure,

00:36:51.030 --> 00:36:54.160
and we just wanted
to build our product.

00:36:54.160 --> 00:36:57.300
And that's why we
chose App Engine, which

00:36:57.300 --> 00:37:01.080
was at the time the only
fully-managed platform

00:37:01.080 --> 00:37:02.500
available.

00:37:02.500 --> 00:37:06.240
So we started the company
with a Java stack,

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:09.090
but we quickly moved to
Python because we loved

00:37:09.090 --> 00:37:12.450
the simplicity of using it.

00:37:12.450 --> 00:37:15.660
And we really like Google
technologies at LumApps,

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:20.070
so we adapted AngularJS
at the same time,

00:37:20.070 --> 00:37:24.720
and we built an open-source
framework which is called LumX.

00:37:24.720 --> 00:37:28.290
It's like material
designed for AngularJS.

00:37:28.290 --> 00:37:31.520
It's open source, so
you should check it out.

00:37:31.520 --> 00:37:34.430
And since then, more
and more companies

00:37:34.430 --> 00:37:36.250
have been using our product.

00:37:36.250 --> 00:37:38.722
Our team has been growing.

00:37:38.722 --> 00:37:42.890
Our App Engine invoice also
has been growing bigger month

00:37:42.890 --> 00:37:46.700
after month, but
that's good news.

00:37:46.700 --> 00:37:50.870
And when Google recommended
us at the end of last year,

00:37:50.870 --> 00:37:56.450
or whether it be customer like
Veolia with 50,000 employees

00:37:56.450 --> 00:37:59.780
went live, we should
have been scared.

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:00.670
Would it work.

00:38:00.670 --> 00:38:04.820
Will the platform run
under such a load?

00:38:04.820 --> 00:38:06.890
The answer is yes.

00:38:06.890 --> 00:38:10.820
And we like the most
boring Go life ever.

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:13.610
So we were just in
front of our computers,

00:38:13.610 --> 00:38:15.530
looking at the platform
going to explode.

00:38:15.530 --> 00:38:18.980
And no, it just works,
and you can go live

00:38:18.980 --> 00:38:22.310
on a huge number of people
and the platform will work.

00:38:25.860 --> 00:38:30.300
So as you can see,
we've been relying

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:34.680
on almost only the standard
App Engine from the beginning,

00:38:34.680 --> 00:38:38.940
and we've been adding
some PCs along the way.

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:43.800
So our backend is in Python,
and we use all the niceties

00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:45.990
from Google App Engine.

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:48.030
So we use the cache system.

00:38:48.030 --> 00:38:51.720
We use the native integration
with Cloud Datastore,

00:38:51.720 --> 00:38:54.030
which is NoSQL database.

00:38:54.030 --> 00:38:58.890
And we use the queuing
system to handle large tasks

00:38:58.890 --> 00:39:03.390
that you want to run
into in the background.

00:39:03.390 --> 00:39:07.250
We also use Cloud Endpoints,
which is a framework

00:39:07.250 --> 00:39:09.740
to build some API.

00:39:09.740 --> 00:39:13.160
So you have all the OS/2
authentication built in.

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:16.610
It's the same technology that
runs the API on all the Google

00:39:16.610 --> 00:39:19.820
products, so Drive, Gmail,
Calendar, and so on.

00:39:19.820 --> 00:39:24.860
And it's used by all our clients
through the web, Android, iOS,

00:39:24.860 --> 00:39:28.610
and all our customers who are
doing some custom integration

00:39:28.610 --> 00:39:32.130
using the API.

00:39:32.130 --> 00:39:36.420
So my diagram here is
not totally accurate

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:39.810
because we have some PCs
running outside of App Engine.

00:39:39.810 --> 00:39:43.150
So for instance, we have a
small cluster of Elasticsearch.

00:39:43.150 --> 00:39:45.600
I will explain after
a while we have this.

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:49.140
And we also have some
machine learning PCs which

00:39:49.140 --> 00:39:52.080
are running on Compute Engine.

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:56.090
And with this, we can interact
with external services--

00:39:56.090 --> 00:40:01.140
so that can be all the Drive
API, Calendar API, Gmail API,

00:40:01.140 --> 00:40:04.860
as well as other third parties.

00:40:04.860 --> 00:40:08.420
We also plan on
integrating with Firebase

00:40:08.420 --> 00:40:12.090
for real-time notification
and directions in the product.

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:20.220
So you can do pretty much
everything with App Engine,

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:24.480
but sometimes when you use
it you will get a roadblock.

00:40:24.480 --> 00:40:26.730
And you have to
ask this question,

00:40:26.730 --> 00:40:29.130
should I move away
from App Engine,

00:40:29.130 --> 00:40:33.330
should I go down the stack to
Container Engine or Compute

00:40:33.330 --> 00:40:34.930
Engine?

00:40:34.930 --> 00:40:38.410
Well this happened to
us at various occasions,

00:40:38.410 --> 00:40:42.430
and we always add to come
up with a pragmatic solution

00:40:42.430 --> 00:40:44.180
to handle these situations.

00:40:44.180 --> 00:40:47.470
So let me tell you the
story of our search engine,

00:40:47.470 --> 00:40:49.300
for instance.

00:40:49.300 --> 00:40:53.890
So it's to build on top of
the App Engine Search API

00:40:53.890 --> 00:40:56.980
and some third party's API.

00:40:56.980 --> 00:41:01.310
So the App Engine Search
API is really great.

00:41:01.310 --> 00:41:04.660
It allows you to index
documents and do complex queries

00:41:04.660 --> 00:41:05.650
at scale.

00:41:05.650 --> 00:41:07.000
It's fully managed.

00:41:07.000 --> 00:41:09.470
And don't get me
wrong, it just works.

00:41:09.470 --> 00:41:13.740
So for us, it was like
the perfect search system.

00:41:13.740 --> 00:41:17.810
So we started using it in
production back in 2012,

00:41:17.810 --> 00:41:21.020
and at this time it was
like an alpha product.

00:41:21.020 --> 00:41:23.260
So it was very, very new.

00:41:23.260 --> 00:41:27.680
But it has always worked
for us except when

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:32.450
you do some very complex queries
which exceed 2,000 bytes.

00:41:32.450 --> 00:41:35.220
And when you do that,
it just doesn't work.

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:38.210
And so this is the limit.

00:41:38.210 --> 00:41:41.000
But we needed more because we
have some very complex queries

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:45.320
to run, but this is a very
small fraction of our queries.

00:41:45.320 --> 00:41:50.420
So the question was, should
we remove the Search API

00:41:50.420 --> 00:41:53.060
for those queries?

00:41:53.060 --> 00:41:55.160
And the answer was no.

00:41:55.160 --> 00:42:00.290
We will just create a
small Elasticsearch cluster

00:42:00.290 --> 00:42:03.500
and index the documents twice.

00:42:03.500 --> 00:42:06.080
And for the small
fraction of queries

00:42:06.080 --> 00:42:08.240
that do not work
with the Search API,

00:42:08.240 --> 00:42:12.500
we will use the Elasticsearch
cluster instead.

00:42:12.500 --> 00:42:15.820
And this cluster runs
on Compute Engine.

00:42:15.820 --> 00:42:18.280
And it's the same with the API.

00:42:18.280 --> 00:42:22.630
So the Cloud Endpoints
framework is really great

00:42:22.630 --> 00:42:28.360
to expose your API and
have a way for people

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:30.280
to interact with it online.

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:35.570
But it doesn't come with a
good documentation system yet,

00:42:35.570 --> 00:42:41.860
so we had to hack our way
into creating such a portal.

00:42:41.860 --> 00:42:45.480
But as the framework for Cloud
Endpoints is open source,

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:50.740
we were able to just
create this portal using

00:42:50.740 --> 00:42:53.830
these open-source libraries.

00:42:53.830 --> 00:42:57.310
And so just a few
tips if you want

00:42:57.310 --> 00:42:59.590
to be using App
Engine efficiently.

00:42:59.590 --> 00:43:02.600
The first thing is that
you should read the manual.

00:43:02.600 --> 00:43:05.350
There are some very good
stuff in the documentation

00:43:05.350 --> 00:43:09.220
from Google, especially
the English version,

00:43:09.220 --> 00:43:12.310
which is more up to date.

00:43:12.310 --> 00:43:16.030
You shouldn't be afraid to use
services that are relatively

00:43:16.030 --> 00:43:18.700
new because they work.

00:43:18.700 --> 00:43:24.880
So we used some service in
alpha mode, and it was just OK.

00:43:24.880 --> 00:43:27.220
You should subscribe to the
mailing list from App Engine

00:43:27.220 --> 00:43:29.094
because there is some
really good stuff here,

00:43:29.094 --> 00:43:32.750
and you can get some
good information.

00:43:32.750 --> 00:43:35.020
And the last point
is not obvious,

00:43:35.020 --> 00:43:39.910
but you should pay for
support because sometimes when

00:43:39.910 --> 00:43:41.980
you are in production you
really have some issues

00:43:41.980 --> 00:43:45.130
and you want the support
team to give you some answers

00:43:45.130 --> 00:43:46.106
very quickly.

00:43:49.530 --> 00:43:51.570
So everything is
great with App Engine,

00:43:51.570 --> 00:43:55.300
but we have a few
challenges today.

00:43:55.300 --> 00:44:00.150
So the first one is about
measuring and debugging.

00:44:00.150 --> 00:44:03.000
So App Engine comes
with Stackdriver,

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:06.930
which are a lot of features
to do some logging,

00:44:06.930 --> 00:44:11.010
some analytics on the
code, some debugging.

00:44:11.010 --> 00:44:12.310
So it's really great.

00:44:12.310 --> 00:44:15.240
It's really good to have access
to all the logging system

00:44:15.240 --> 00:44:18.240
and to be able to
search inside the logs.

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:20.380
But sometimes, you need more.

00:44:20.380 --> 00:44:24.180
You need to have an agent
installed on the machine,

00:44:24.180 --> 00:44:28.350
and you cannot
this with standard.

00:44:28.350 --> 00:44:29.990
So for instance,
if you want to use

00:44:29.990 --> 00:44:32.760
a tool like [INAUDIBLE]
or Duck Duck Duck,

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:35.520
you won't be able to do it
with Google App Engine standard

00:44:35.520 --> 00:44:39.270
and you will have
to move to Flex.

00:44:39.270 --> 00:44:43.140
Then we have some, I
wouldn't say issues,

00:44:43.140 --> 00:44:45.480
but sometimes it can
be tricky to have

00:44:45.480 --> 00:44:50.820
your application working in some
countries, especially in China

00:44:50.820 --> 00:44:58.480
where App Engine is not the best
tool over in these countries.

00:44:58.480 --> 00:45:02.230
You have some quotas also.

00:45:02.230 --> 00:45:06.970
So for instance, if you intend
to send a very large number

00:45:06.970 --> 00:45:09.820
of emails, you will be
limited on App Engine

00:45:09.820 --> 00:45:13.660
because they want to
prevent spam and so on.

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:16.480
So keep in mind those quotas.

00:45:16.480 --> 00:45:19.360
And if you need more,
you should consider

00:45:19.360 --> 00:45:23.500
using an Excel service instead.

00:45:23.500 --> 00:45:26.590
For the database, you
have Cloud Datastore,

00:45:26.590 --> 00:45:32.170
which is really good tool, but
you need to know how to use it.

00:45:32.170 --> 00:45:36.175
Because, if you use
it as a SQL database,

00:45:36.175 --> 00:45:38.210
it will just not work.

00:45:38.210 --> 00:45:42.510
So keep in mind that
it's designed for scale

00:45:42.510 --> 00:45:44.800
and that there is
some trade-off when

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:46.900
you design your data model.

00:45:46.900 --> 00:45:49.060
And if you do that,
you can really

00:45:49.060 --> 00:45:55.300
have something which
will scale with no limit.

00:45:55.300 --> 00:45:59.500
And the last point is about
moving from standard to Flex.

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:04.300
So as Brian was
saying, if you want

00:46:04.300 --> 00:46:06.580
to move from one to
another, you need

00:46:06.580 --> 00:46:09.350
to really decouple
the services that you

00:46:09.350 --> 00:46:11.920
are using because
natively on standard you

00:46:11.920 --> 00:46:17.980
can use the cache
system, the queue system,

00:46:17.980 --> 00:46:18.940
and the Datastore.

00:46:18.940 --> 00:46:22.540
And you cannot do this directly
using the same libraries

00:46:22.540 --> 00:46:23.470
on Flex .

00:46:23.470 --> 00:46:27.640
So there are some
trade-offs on how you

00:46:27.640 --> 00:46:30.570
can move from one to the other.

00:46:34.030 --> 00:46:38.130
And finally, let's talk
about the new stuff

00:46:38.130 --> 00:46:40.450
that we are working on.

00:46:40.450 --> 00:46:42.900
So the first one
is Cloud Spanner,

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:45.130
and this one is really exciting.

00:46:45.130 --> 00:46:48.430
It offers the scalability
of a managed database

00:46:48.430 --> 00:46:52.110
but with the flexibility of SQL.

00:46:52.110 --> 00:46:57.940
So we will implement it for
complex objects, relationships

00:46:57.940 --> 00:47:01.750
where the Cloud Datastore is
maybe too limited for this--

00:47:01.750 --> 00:47:03.720
it's not the right tool.

00:47:03.720 --> 00:47:09.450
We also want to implement a
multiregion infrastructure

00:47:09.450 --> 00:47:14.510
because we have instances in
multiple regions in App Engine.

00:47:14.510 --> 00:47:18.720
And natively, there
is no load balancer

00:47:18.720 --> 00:47:22.920
on top of all these regions, so
you have to do it on your own.

00:47:22.920 --> 00:47:24.600
And so we are looking
at the best way

00:47:24.600 --> 00:47:28.130
to aggregate all
of the instances

00:47:28.130 --> 00:47:31.420
under the same infrastructure.

00:47:31.420 --> 00:47:34.650
And finally, so our
dream at LumApps

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:38.500
is to build a personal
assistant for employees

00:47:38.500 --> 00:47:41.530
and to be proactive to
serve the right information

00:47:41.530 --> 00:47:43.450
to the right people
at the right time.

00:47:43.450 --> 00:47:46.870
And to do that, we've started
implementing machine learning

00:47:46.870 --> 00:47:50.920
algorithms, and we want
to intensify the usage

00:47:50.920 --> 00:47:55.420
and build on top of Cloud
ML to scale our models

00:47:55.420 --> 00:47:56.890
and refine our data.

00:47:59.500 --> 00:48:04.530
And so, finally, when I
prepared for this presentation,

00:48:04.530 --> 00:48:05.790
Brian asked me a question.

00:48:05.790 --> 00:48:09.600
He said, if you had to
make the decision today,

00:48:09.600 --> 00:48:12.340
would you make
the same decision?

00:48:12.340 --> 00:48:13.480
And the answer is yes.

00:48:16.590 --> 00:48:19.934
App Engine has been really
helpful along the way.

00:48:19.934 --> 00:48:23.860
And frankly, I'm not sure
I would be here today

00:48:23.860 --> 00:48:27.020
if we had not made this
choice five years ago.

00:48:27.020 --> 00:48:31.480
So today, we have 1.5 million
users with only one person

00:48:31.480 --> 00:48:34.540
doing some DevOps in the team.

00:48:34.540 --> 00:48:37.690
So we were able to
really focus on building

00:48:37.690 --> 00:48:40.950
the product, which is great.

00:48:40.950 --> 00:48:43.470
What we could have
done better is

00:48:43.470 --> 00:48:46.900
the decoupling between
standard App Engine features

00:48:46.900 --> 00:48:49.990
to make sure that it's
easy to switch from one

00:48:49.990 --> 00:48:53.700
system to another, so from
standard to flex to contain

00:48:53.700 --> 00:48:56.940
Container Engine and
to Compute Engine.

00:48:56.940 --> 00:48:57.440
That's all.

00:48:57.440 --> 00:48:58.280
Thank you.

00:48:58.280 --> 00:49:02.480
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:02.480 --> 00:49:03.730
as

